Annotations: |
  This cell is salt/utils — a flat directory of ~170 independent helper modules (data.py,
  files.py, platform.py, args.py, dictupdate.py, network.py, dateutils.py,
  atomicfile.py, plus many narrow third-party-integration helpers like boto3mod.py,
  mattermost.py, nxos.py, icinga2.py). It has no single cohesive API of its own: each file
  is imported directly by name (import salt.utils.data, import salt.utils.files, ...) from
  execution modules, state modules, runners, the loader itself, and nearly every other part of
  the codebase. This CODEMANIFEST documents a representative sample of 5 widely-depended-upon
  functions from 5 of the least domain-specific files, not an exhaustive catalog of the
  directory's ~170 files.

  A second, narrower slice of this same directory is also loaded dynamically as the __utils__
  dict: salt.loader.utils() builds a LazyLoader (tag utils) over this same directory so
  execution/state modules can call __utils__['<name>.<func>'] instead of a static Python
  import. Both access paths (static import salt.utils.X and dynamic __utils__['X.func'])
  resolve to the same on-disk files.

---

"update(dest: dict[str, object], upd: dict[str, object], recursive_update: bool, merge_lists: bool, strict: bool) -> dest:dict[str, object]":
  location: dictupdate.py
  annotations: |
    Recursively merges `upd` into `dest` and returns `dest`. Salt's config-merging primitive:
    used to combine multiple config files, pillar layers, and loader option overrides into one
    dict.

    `dest`: destination mapping, mutated and returned
    `upd`: mapping whose keys are merged into `dest`
    `recursive_update`: when true (the default), nested dict values are merged key-by-key instead
    of the shallower value in `upd` replacing the one in `dest` wholesale
    `merge_lists`: when true, list values under the same key are concatenated (dest + upd,
    de-duplicated) instead of `upd`'s list replacing `dest`'s
    `strict`: when true, raise instead of silently falling back if a list is merged against a
    mapping while `merge_lists` is true

    Algorithm:
    1. If neither mapping shares any top-level key, treat the update as a plain replacement
    2. Otherwise, for each key in `upd`: if both sides are mappings, recurse; if both sides are
       lists and `merge_lists` is true, concatenate with de-duplication; otherwise `upd`'s value
       replaces `dest`'s
    3. Return the mutated `dest`

"compare_dicts(old: dict[str, object], new: dict[str, object]) -> changes:dict[str, object]":
  location: data.py
  annotations: |
    Diff two dicts (typically before/after state values) into a change-description dict.

    `old`: mapping before the change
    `new`: mapping after the change
    `changes`: for every key present in either mapping whose value differs, a nested
    `{"old": ..., "new": ...}` entry; keys with identical values in both mappings are omitted

"decode(data: object, encoding: str | None, errors: str, keep: bool, normalize: bool, preserve_dict_class: bool, preserve_tuples: bool, to_str: bool) -> value:object":
  location: data.py
  annotations: |
    Recursively walks a nested structure (dict/list/tuple/bytes/str) and decodes every bytes
    value to `str`, used pervasively to normalize data crossing process/transport boundaries
    (wire payloads, subprocess output, grains) into consistent text.

    `data`: value to recursively decode; may be a scalar, or a dict/list/tuple containing more of
    the same
    `encoding`: target text encoding; when omitted, Salt's configured default is used
    `errors`: decode error-handling mode passed to the underlying bytes.decode call
    `keep`: when true, values that fail to decode are kept as-is instead of raising
    `normalize`: when true, applies Unicode normalization to decoded strings
    `preserve_dict_class`: when true, the original mapping type is preserved instead of coercing
    to plain dict
    `preserve_tuples`: when true, tuple values are kept as tuples instead of becoming list

"is_windows() -> result:bool":
  location: platform.py
  annotations: |
    Reports whether the current process is running on Windows. Representative of a whole family
    of OS-detection predicates in this file (is_linux, is_darwin, is_freebsd, is_proxy,
    is_smartos_globalzone, ...) that __virtual__() functions across salt/modules and
    salt/grains call to decide whether a platform-specific module should load at all.

"clean_kwargs(...kwargs: object) -> kwargs:dict[str, object]":
  location: args.py
  annotations: |
    Strips Salt's internal __pub_* bookkeeping keys (job metadata Salt attaches to every
    published call) from a kwargs mapping before it is handed to a module or state function that
    does not expect them.

    `kwargs`: raw keyword-argument mapping as received from the publish layer
    `kwargs` (return): the same mapping with every __pub_*-prefixed key removed

"fopen(...args: object, ...kwargs: object) -> file_obj:IO":
  location: files.py
  annotations: |
    Salt's cross-platform replacement for the builtin open(): normalizes newline handling and
    encoding defaults consistently across POSIX/Windows and Python versions. Used throughout the
    codebase instead of calling open() directly whenever a file is read or written on behalf of
    a state or module.

    `args`: positional arguments forwarded to the underlying open() call (path, mode, ...)
    `kwargs`: keyword arguments forwarded to the underlying open() call

